From: Zack Maril <zmaril@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:48:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] headstart: report a unit's output only once its
 dependencies succeed

With `-Zheadstart`, a unit can start on a dependency's early metadata, and
the dependency can still fail afterwards: its function bodies are checked
after the early metadata is written. If it fails, the units started early
would report diagnostics, or a result, that the build without
`-Zheadstart` never gets to.

A unit that starts while any of its dependencies hasn't finished cleanly
is now provisional: its diagnostics, JSON messages and result are held
until every dependency has finished cleanly, then emitted in their
original order. If a dependency fails, they're dropped, along with those
of units depending on it. A held unit still frees its job slot and
unblocks its dependents when it finishes; only the report waits.

So a failing build prints the same diagnostics, JSON messages and exit
status as without `-Zheadstart`. What can differ is output that already
depends on timing: progress lines, "waiting for other jobs", and the order
of JSON messages across crates.

A scrape-examples unit that is allowed to fail is treated as having
succeeded, as it is when it isn't held.

